# Article: Project

This project is `generic-scraper`: a configurable scraping component that a
distributed test/job orchestrator runs across worker nodes. A caller supplies a
`ScraperType` configuration — engine, browser, processing type, proxy, retry
policy — and gets back parsed documents, without writing engine-specific code.

## Naming collision — read this first

The `.feature` files say "Swarm Forge" to mean **the distributed job
orchestrator this scraper runs under**: workers, shards, artifact stores, node
affinity. That is the product being specified.

You are also, separately, running inside SwarmForge the agent tool. They are
unrelated. Never confuse the two, and never write code that talks to the agent
tool. When a scenario says "worker node" or "orchestrator", that is the
scraper's runtime environment and it is faked in tests.

## Swarm

Six roles, all on the `claude` backend (Claude Code). Do not switch backends or
edit `swarmforge/swarmforge.conf`. The hardening role is spelled `hardender`.

## Layout

```
swarmforge/              role prompts, constitution, swarm config
.swarmforge/             swarm state -- never edit by hand
.worktrees/<role>/       one git worktree per role -- stay in your own
features/                hand-written Gherkin -- the source of truth
src/generic_scraper/     library code
tests/                   unit tests, mirroring the src layout
tests/acceptance/steps/  pytest-bdd step definitions for features/
fixtures/                saved HTML/JSON pages and configs the tests use
docs/                    usage documentation
```

## Acceptance specs (Gherkin)

This project is spec-first. The eight `.feature` files in `features/` are
hand-written by the user, numbered in intended build order, and are the source
of truth for behavior.

- Step definitions live in `tests/acceptance/steps/`, run by `pytest-bdd`, so
  there is exactly one test runner. `pytest` alone runs everything.
- The specifier does **not** rewrite these files wholesale. It reviews them,
  normalizes Gherkin conventions, prunes example-table columns that do not
  change behavior, consolidates repeated setup into `Background`, raises
  anything ambiguous or untestable with the user, and then cuts a slice.
- The coder implements against named scenarios. Nobody edits a `.feature` to
  make a failing scenario pass. If a scenario is wrong, hand it back to the
  specifier with the scenario name and why.

Build in feature-file order (01 through 08) unless the specifier says
otherwise. Each slice is a feature or a few scenarios of one feature.

## Domain model

- **ScraperType** — the configuration object. Keys seen so far:
  `scraper_engine`, `browser_type`, `processing_type`, `secondary`,
  `use_proxy`, `proxy_url`, `proxy_port`, `proxy_pass_key`, `proxy_pass_val`,
  plus retry policy. Treat the feature files as authoritative on key names;
  do not invent or rename keys.
- **Engines** — `requests`, `playwright`, `selenium`. One engine interface;
  each engine is an adapter behind it.
- **Processing types** — `beautifulsoup`, `lxml`, `html.parser`, `regex`.
  One parser interface; each is an adapter.
- **Defaults and fallbacks** — `requests` is the default engine, no browser
  configured. An unavailable primary engine falls back to the configured
  `secondary`, or to `requests`. Failing that, a descriptive error naming what
  was tried.
- **Distribution** — sharding across workers, artifact upload to a job store,
  node affinity and resource constraints. The orchestrator is an interface this
  project codes against, not one it implements.

## Engineering constraints

- Python 3.11+. Allowed dependencies: `requests`, `beautifulsoup4`, `lxml`,
  `PyYAML`, `playwright`, `selenium`, `pytest`, `pytest-bdd`, `ruff`, `mypy`.
  Anything else needs the architect's agreement first.
- Engines and parsers sit behind narrow interfaces with a registry. Adding a
  fourth engine must not require touching the other three.
- **No network in tests. Ever.** No real HTTP, no real browser launch, no real
  worker node. Engines are exercised through an injected fake transport or a
  fake driver at the adapter boundary; the orchestrator is a test double.
  A test that reaches the live internet or starts a browser is a defect, and
  the hardender should kill it.
- Type hints on every public function. `ruff` clean, `mypy src` clean.
- Public behavior is documented in `docs/` in the same slice that ships it.

## Guardrails

Proxy support, custom headers, and retry policy are legitimate infrastructure
features and are in scope — they are how a job runs from inside a network.

What is not in scope, and must be refused rather than improvised: rotating
proxies or randomizing fingerprints in order to defeat bot detection; solving
or bypassing captchas; scraping behind a login the caller is not authorized to
use; ignoring `robots.txt` by default; or collecting personal data. If a
handoff asks for one of these, refuse it, say why in a note back to the
specifier, and stop.

Secrets — `proxy_pass_val` and anything like it — are never logged, never
written to an artifact, and never committed as a real value. Fixtures use
obvious dummies.

## Communication

Handoffs are short and direct: what changed, what remains, what you need from
the next role. No verification transcripts. No commentary about your own
process.

## Governance

Each role owns its own prompt and workflow. Do not edit another role's prompt,
`swarmforge.conf`, or the constitution without explicit authorization from the
user.
